375, 0.03271484375, -0.0184173583984375, 0.0218353271484375, 0.0021514892578125, -0.00249481201171875, 0.03790283203125, 0.025177001953125, -0.007762908935546875, 0.01375579833984375, -0.003498077392578125, 0.041717529296875, -0.00494384765625, -0.00450897216796875, 0.0177154541015625, 0.0382080078125, 0.044281005859375, -0.001102447509765625, -0.022613525390625, 0.019683837890625, 0.0032062530517578125, -0.00606536865234375, -0.0243377685546875, 0.01099395751953125, -0.0235595703125, 0.01031494140625, 0.01058197021484375, 0.029815673828125, 0.00820159912109375, -0.0052642822265625, 0.0228118896484375, -0.0130157470703125, -0.0169830322265625, -0.01451873779296875, 0.03363037109375, 0.0020046234130859375, 0.0017194747924804688, 0.02813720703125, -0.01509857177734375, 0.00792694091796875, -0.02215576171875, 0.006801605224609375, -0.0233917236328125, 0.01500701904296875, 0.01134490966796875, -0.02374267578125, -0.0014715194702148438, -0.005069732666015625, -0.002262115478515625, -0.014068603515625, -0.013641357421875, 0.007534027099609375, 0.01145172119140625, 0.0093536376953125, -0.0273895263671875, -0.004497528076171875, 0.0003101825714111328, 0.00958251953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**Introduction**\n\nThis document provides a beginner-friendly introduction to the basic rules and objectives of chess, a classic two-player board game renowned for its strategy and skill.\n\n**Setup**\n\nChess is played on a square board with 64 squares arranged in an 8\u00d78 grid, alternating light and dark colors. Each player controls sixteen pieces: one king, one queen, two rooks, two bishops, two knights, and eight pawns. The board is set up so that each player has a white (or light) square on their right-hand corner. The second rank (row) from each player is filled with pawns. On the first rank, from left to right, the pieces are arranged as: rook, knight, bishop, queen, king, bishop, knight, rook. The queen is placed on the square matching her color (white queen on a white square, black queen on a black square). White's pieces occupy the first two ranks, and Black's occupy the last two ranks. White always moves first, and players alternate turns.\n\n**Piece Movement**\n\nEach type of piece moves in a unique way, and only one piece may occupy a square at any time. Capturing is optional and occurs when a piece moves onto a square occupied by an opponent's piece, removing it from the board.\n\n- **King:** Moves one square in any direction (horizontally, vertically, or diagonally). The king has a special move called *castling*, where it moves two squares towards a rook, and the rook moves to the square next to the king. Castling can only occur if neither piece has moved, the squares between them are unoccupied, and the king is not in, moving through, or ending up in check.\n- **Queen:** Moves any number of squares in any direction (horizontally, vertically, or diagonally).\n- **Rook:** Moves any number of squares horizontally or vertically.\n- **Bishop:** Moves any number of squares diagonally.\n- **Knight:** Moves in an L-shape: two squares in one direction and then one square perpendicular. Knights can jump over other pieces.\n- **Pawn:** Moves forward one square, but captures diagonally forward one square. On its first move, a pawn may move forward two squares. Pawns have two special moves: *en passant* (a special capture when an opposing pawn moves two squares forward from its starting position and lands beside the player's pawn) and *promotion* (when a pawn reaches the farthest rank, it is promoted to any other piece, usually a queen).\n\n**Objective and Check**\n\nThe main objective is to checkmate the opponent's king. A king is in *check* when it is under immediate threat of capture by an opponent's piece. The player must make a move to remove the check. *Checkmate* occurs when the king is in check and there is no legal move to escape the threat; the game ends immediately.\n\n**Draws**\n\nA game can also end in a draw (tie) in several ways:\n- **Stalemate:** The player to move has no legal moves and is not in check.\n- **Insufficient material:** Neither player has enough pieces to force checkmate (e.g., only kings remain).\n- **Threefold repetition:** The same position occurs three times with the same player to move and the same possible moves.\n- **Fifty-move rule:** Fifty consecutive moves have occurred without any pawn movement or piece capture.\n\n**Additional Notes**\n\nWhile not covered in detail here, chess is often played with time controls (limits on how long each player can take for their moves), and games are recorded using chess notation. Players are also expected to follow good etiquette, such as shaking hands before and after the game."}}